<Layout title="What is HDR? Dolby Vision, HDR10, HLG Explained — iPhone vs Camera (2026)" description="HDR kya hota hai aur iPhone/camera mein kaise kaam karta hai? Dolby Vision, HDR10, HDR10+ aur HLG ka difference, benefits, aur HDR vs Log ka confusion clear karta ye guide.">



<section class="hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="tag">DELIVERY FORMATS</span>
    <h1>What is HDR? Dolby Vision, HDR10 &amp; HLG — iPhone vs Camera</h1>
    <p class="dek">Har naya iPhone "Dolby Vision HDR" record karne ka daava karta hai. Lekin yeh Log se kaise different hai, aur camera pe HDR kaise kaam karta hai? Poori clarity yahaan.</p>
    <div class="meta-row">
      <span>By Ajay K Meena</span>
      <span>Updated: July 2026</span>
      <span>9 min read</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<article class="wrap">

  <p>Sabse pehle ek confusion clear karte hain jo 90% editors ko hoti hai: <strong>HDR aur Log same cheez nahi hain.</strong> Log ek acquisition tool hai (grading ke liye flat image), HDR ek delivery/display standard hai (final image ko sahi brightness ke saath screen pe dikhana). Ab detail mein samajhte hain.</p>

  <h2>HDR Kya Hai?</h2>
  <p>HDR (High Dynamic Range) ek display aur delivery standard hai jo <strong>brightness range aur color volume</strong> ko normal SDR (Standard Dynamic Range) se kaafi zyada expand karta hai. SDR displays typically 100 nits peak brightness pe capped hote hain. HDR displays 600 se 4000+ nits tak jaa sakte hain — matlab highlights zyada bright dikhte hain bina detail khoye, aur blacks zyada deep dikhte hain.</p>

  <div class="diagram">
    <div class="diagram-title">PEAK BRIGHTNESS: SDR vs HDR FORMATS (NITS)</div>
    <div class="nit-bars">
      <div class="nit-col"><div class="nit-bar" style="height:12%"><div class="nit-val">100</div></div><div class="nit-name">SDR<br>(Rec.709)</div></div>
      <div class="nit-col"><div class="nit-bar" style="height:45%"><div class="nit-val">400</div></div><div class="nit-name">HLG<br>(typical)</div></div>
      <div class="nit-col"><div class="nit-bar" style="height:70%"><div class="nit-val">1000</div></div><div class="nit-name">HDR10<br>(mastering)</div></div>
      <div class="nit-col"><div class="nit-bar" style="height:100%"><div class="nit-val">4000+</div></div><div class="nit-name">Dolby Vision<br>(mastering)</div></div>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:12.5px;color:var(--ink-soft);margin:8px 0 0;">Yeh mastering-range figures hain — actual consumer display brightness inse kam hoti hai.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>HDR Ke 4 Main Formats</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Metadata</th><th>Bit Depth</th><th>Kaun Use Karta Hai</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>HDR10</b></td><td>Static (poori video ke liye ek value)</td><td>10-bit</td><td>Open standard — sabse zyada supported</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>HDR10+</b></td><td>Dynamic (scene-by-scene)</td><td>10-bit</td><td>Samsung, Amazon Prime Video</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Dolby Vision</b></td><td>Dynamic (frame-by-frame)</td><td>10 to 12-bit</td><td>Apple, Netflix, licensed — most precise</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>HLG</b> (Hybrid Log Gamma)</td><td>No metadata needed</td><td>10-bit</td><td>Broadcast (BBC/NHK developed) — SDR-backward-compatible</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><strong>HLG sabse practical hai broadcast ke liye</strong> kyunki isko koi metadata nahi chahiye — same signal SDR display pe bhi dekh sakte ho aur HDR display pe bhi, without a separate SDR version banaye. Isiliye YouTube HDR pipeline mein HLG ka role important hai.</p>

  <h2>iPhone Mein HDR Kaise Kaam Karta Hai</h2>
  <div class="brand-card">
    <h4>Dolby Vision Profile 8.4</h4>
    <p>iPhone 12 se lekar latest models tak, jab bhi "HDR Video" setting on hoti hai (Settings → Camera → Record Video), iPhone <strong>Dolby Vision Profile 8.4</strong> mein record karta hai — yeh 10-bit HEVC codec use karta hai aur HLG ke saath cross-compatible hai. Har frame ka apna dynamic metadata hota hai, jo capture ke time hi camera analyze karke embed karta hai — matlab tone-mapping automatically ho jaati hai per-scene basis pe.</p>
    <span class="spec-chip">10-bit HEVC</span><span class="spec-chip">Per-frame metadata</span><span class="spec-chip">iPhone 12+</span>
  </div>
  <div class="callout"><b>Important distinction:</b> Yeh Dolby Vision HDR record mode <em>Apple Log</em> se bilkul alag hai. Dolby Vision ek "baked-in," ready-to-view HDR result deta hai. Apple Log (iPhone 15 Pro+ only, Final Cut Camera app se) ek flat, ungraded image deta hai jo manual grading ke liye designed hai — zyada control, zyada kaam.</div>

  <h2>Camera Mein HDR Kaise Kaam Karta Hai</h2>
  <p>Mirrorless aur cinema cameras mein HDR do tareeke se milta hai:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>HLG Picture Profile:</strong> Sony aur Panasonic bodies mein directly HLG gamma select kar sakte ho — yeh "ready to grade lightly" image deta hai jo Log se zyada contrasty hoti hai but SDR-compatible bhi rehti hai.</li>
    <li><strong>HDR-PQ Mode (Canon):</strong> Kuch Canon bodies mein HDR PQ (Perceptual Quantizer) mode hota hai jo directly Rec.2100-PQ standard mein record karta hai — mostly straight-to-delivery HDR content ke liye, grading ke liye nahi.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>HDR vs Log — Side-by-Side</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Log</th><th>HDR (Dolby Vision/HDR10/HLG)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Acquisition — max dynamic range capture</td><td>Delivery — display pe accurate luminance</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Appearance</td><td>Flat, desaturated, ungraded</td><td>Final, graded/tone-mapped, ready-to-view</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Needs grading?</td><td>Haan, mandatory</td><td>Nahi, ya minimal (already mapped)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Where used</td><td>Camera recording stage</td><td>Final export/delivery stage</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Benefits of HDR</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Highlights bina clip hue bright dikhte hain (sun, lights, reflections)</li>
    <li>Deeper, richer blacks — better perceived contrast</li>
    <li>Wider color volume — saturated colors bright areas mein bhi maintain hote hain</li>
    <li>Real-world jaisa viewing experience compatible displays pe</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Practical Trap: YouTube HDR Upload</h2>
  <p>Jab tum HDR video YouTube pe upload karte ho, platform automatically ek <strong>SDR trim-pass</strong> generate karta hai taaki normal screens pe bhi video theek dikhe. Agar tum control chahte ho ki SDR version kaisa dikhega, toh khud LUT ya trim pass supply karo export ke time — warna YouTube ka auto-conversion kabhi flat, kabhi over-saturated result de sakta hai.</p>

  <h2>FAQs</h2>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>HDR aur Log profile same hain kya?</h3>
    <p>Nahi. Log ek acquisition curve hai jo grading ke liye flat image deta hai. HDR ek delivery/display standard hai jo final graded image ko wide luminance range ke saath screen pe accurately dikhata hai. Dono alag purpose serve karte hain.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>iPhone HDR video record karta hai matlab kya?</h3>
    <p>iPhone 12 aur uske baad ke models Dolby Vision Profile 8.4 mein record karte hain — yeh HLG ke saath backward-compatible hai aur per-frame dynamic metadata carry karta hai jo compatible displays pe automatically apply hoti hai.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>YouTube pe HDR video upload karne se kya hota hai SDR viewers ke liye?</h3>
    <p>YouTube automatically ek SDR trim-pass generate karta hai HDR upload se, taaki normal screens pe bhi video sahi dikhe. Aap khud ka SDR trim ya LUT bhi supply kar sakte ho control ke liye.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="cta">
    <h2>HDR aur Log Dono Ka Workflow Ek Saath Seekho</h2>
